Hi. I'm wondering, what relatively simple evolutionary tweaks and
features would you like to see added to NetHack?

I feel like adding a few things without making any major gameplay
changes. Currently, a new set of objects - box of {pixie dust/grit/
baking soda} - and a new "gentleman/lady adventurer" player role. For
the former, sprinkling pixie dust on an object would increase or
otherwise favorably/unfavorably alter its magical properties - add 1-3
points to a ring of protection, increase the range/power of a wand,
add a random resistance to armor, for example. For the latter, think
of a hero from a Victorian-era Wells, Verne, etc. novel; I'd like to
play a role with a more even balance between spellcasting and magic
than the current roles allow. Think of the role as a lawful/neutral
jack-of-all-trades, a crafty swashbuckler, professor who can do a
little fencing, knight-errant, a tinkerer, Doc Savage, so on. I'll
also probably add one or two minor dungeon branch levels. Or maybe a
more significant branch. The offices of the gnomes' Jewelry Cartel
(analogous to Ft Ludios)? A branch of open random underground caverns
a la Journey to the Center of the Earth, with trees, mushrooms, lakes?
Maybe I could add something like a grenade - an "iron ball" that you
can light with a lamp, lit potion of oil, whatever. ("The little dog
catches the grenade. The little dog drops a grenade. The grenade
explodes. You are blasted by fragments.") A way to create a potion a
la a magic marker? (Chemistry/alchemy set? Maybe that would be an
avocation for the Gentleman Adventurer.) I dunno.

I don't have any desire to make serious revisions to the combat/spell/
role/ascension/whatever system or feel of the game - I think it's well
balanced. However, I think it would be fun to see a new wrinkle or
three added from time to time. A new one of this, one of that, so on.
A little whimsical to fit in with the existing tenor of the game.

I'm happy to do it for my own amusement, but if you folks have
suggestions, things that should be tweaked or fixed, or so on, none of
which are radical or too numerous, post away. If anything comes of it,
I'll figure out how to get it out there. If the (ages old) Sourceforge
project isn't an option, I'll come up with something else.

Cheers, -j



